# XSH sigqueue()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.001
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       sigqueue() to a child with SA_SIGINFO on SIGRTMIN returns 0, and the
           child's handler gets si_code SI_QUEUE, si_value.sival_int 4321
           and si_pid the sender's process ID.
test       t/001-value.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.002
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#ERRORS/ESRCH
class      required
since      2008
says       sigqueue() with signal 0 returns 0 for a live child and sends it
           nothing; for the ID of an exited group leader whose group lives
           on (an ID that cannot be reused yet) it fails with ESRCH.
test       t/002-null.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.003
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       In a single-threaded process, sigqueue(getpid(), SIGRTMIN) with
           SIGRTMIN unblocked and caught runs the handler before sigqueue()
           returns.
test       t/003-self-before-return.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.004
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN+2 and then SIGRTMIN+1 queued while blocked, unblocking
           both at once delivers SIGRTMIN+1 first.
note       Each handler's sa_mask blocks both signals: without that, the
           second signal is delivered on top of the first handler before
           it runs, so the handler bodies run in the reverse order,
           which conforms.
nomutant   the delivery order is the kernel's
test       t/004-lowest-first.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.005
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2008
says       Queuing SIGRTMIN to the caller (blocked, SA_SIGINFO set) with
           sigqueue() fails with EAGAIN no later than the attempt after
           {SIGQUEUE_MAX} signals are queued.
timeout    120
note       {SIGQUEUE_MAX} from sysconf(); over 1000000 the test is
           UNRESOLVED. A system-wide limit may make it fail earlier,
           which is allowed. musl gives -1 for {SIGQUEUE_MAX} (no
           definite limit, which is allowed), so there the test is
           NOTINUSE.
test       t/005-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.006
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2008
says       sigqueue() with signal number -1 or 100000 fails with EINVAL.
test       t/006-einval.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.007
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#ERRORS/EPERM
class      required
since      2008
says       A process running as user 65534 that queues SIGRTMIN to its parent
           (user 0) fails with EPERM, and nothing is delivered.
needs      root
test       t/007-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.008
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       sigqueue() of SIGRTMIN to a child stopped by SIGSTOP returns 0
           within 500 ms.
test       t/008-stopped.c

assertion  XSH.sigqueue.009
anchor     functions/sigqueue.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN caught by a one-argument handler (no SA_SIGINFO) and
           blocked, two sigqueue() calls to the process itself succeed, and
           on unblocking the handler runs at least once.
test       t/009-without-siginfo.c
